WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 38: Seward's Folly and the Klondike Gold Rush

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.440
[Rebecca] In 1867, the United States was still recovering from the catastrophic devastation of the Civil War.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.600
[Rebecca] When Secretary of State William H.

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:27.920
[Rebecca] Seward announced that he had negotiated a treaty to buy nearly 600,000 square miles of frozen subarctic wilderness from Tsar Alexander II of Russia for $7.2 million.

6
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.800
[Rebecca] The American press exploded with mockery.

7
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:38.480
[Rebecca] Editors ridiculed the purchase as Seward's Folly, Seward's icebox, and President Andrew Johnson's polar bear garden.

8
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.080
[Rebecca] For 30 years, Alaska was largely neglected, treated as a desolate frozen wasteland.

9
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:59.600
[Rebecca] But in July 1897, a steamship docked in Seattle carrying a ton of pure gold nuggets from the Yukon, triggering the wildest, most grueling gold rush the world had ever seen, and proving that Seward had pulled off the greatest real estate bargain in human history.

10
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

11
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:24.640
[George] Following Russia's humiliating defeat in the Crimean War, the Tsarist Treasury was depleted, and Russia's far-flung colony in North America, known as Russian America, was a financial drain.

12
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:40.080
[George] With the sea otter population hunted nearly to extinction, the Russian American company was losing money, and Russian military planners feared that their British rivals in Canada would seize the territory in any future conflict without Russia being able to defend it.

13
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.080
[George] In Washington, United States Secretary of State William H.

14
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:52.000
[George] Seward was a passionate, far-sighted expansionist who believed America's destiny lay in commercial dominance of the Pacific Ocean.

15
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] When Russian envoy Eduard De Stokal approached Seward with an offer to sell, Seward seized the opportunity with electrifying speed.

16
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:11.240
[George] Working through the night by the light of gas lamps at the State Department, the two diplomats hammered out a treaty that was signed at 4 o'clock in the morning on March 30, 1867.

17
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] For the sum of $7,200,000, roughly two cents an acre, the United States acquired 586,000 square miles of land, doubling the coastline of the nation.

18
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:33.560
[George] Russian fur traders had exhausted the coastal sea otter populations and found the remote territory financially unsustainable, leading Tsar Alexander I.

19
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:46.280
[George] When news of the Midnight Treaty reached the public, a storm of fierce political satire and ridicule erupted across the American press.

20
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:56.440
[George] Opponents of President Andrew Johnson's deeply unpopular administration viewed the purchase as an outrageous waste of taxpayer money during post-Civil War reconstruction.

21
00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:04.840
[George] Influential newspapers like the New York Tribune branded the acquisition Seward's Folly, Seward's Icebox, and Wallrussia.

22
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:14.040
[George] Cartoonists drew satirical sketches showing Uncle Sam purchasing caged polar bears, giant icebergs, and fields of snow from Russian diplomats.

23
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:23.400
[George] Critics demanded, why should the American taxpayer spend millions for a frozen, dark desert where no crops could grow and where no white man could live?

24
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.800
[George] Yet Seward, supported by powerful Senate foreign relations chairman Charles Sumner, held firm.

25
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:44.120
[George] In a masterful three-hour speech before the Senate, Sumner documented Alaska's vast natural resources, boundless fisheries of salmon and cod, endless virgin forests of spruce and hemlock, and untapped mineral wealth.

26
00:03:44.160 --> 00:04:00.320
[George] On April 9, 1867, the Senate ratified the treaty by a decisive vote of 37 to 2, and on October 18, 1867, the Russian double-headed Eagle flag was lowered in Sitka, replaced by the stars and stripes.

27
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:14.720
[George] Critics in Congress ridiculed Secretary Seward in blistering speeches, claiming that $7 million was being wasted on polar bears, walruses, and endless icebergs with zero economic return for the American taxpayer.

28
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:23.920
[George] For nearly three decades following the transfer, Alaska languished in quiet obscurity, governed loosely by the Navy and the Treasury Department.

29
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.640
[George] Seward passed away in 1872, never seeing his frozen purchase vindicated during his lifetime.

30
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.240
[George] Then, in the summer of 1897, the world was shaken by an economic earthquake.

31
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:46.160
[George] On July 17, 1897, the steamship Portland docked at Schwabacher's Wharf in Seattle, having sailed south from the mouth of the Yukon River.

32
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:58.960
[George] Down the wooden gangplank walked 68 weatherbeaten, bearded prospectors clad in makinas and fur boots, hauling heavy wooden sea chests, buckskin pouches, and milk cans filled with pure gold.

33
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:06.080
[George] The Seattle Post Intelligencer published a sensational headline that flashed across telegraph wires around the globe.

34
00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.160
[George] Gold, gold, gold, gold, 68 rich men on the steamer Portland.

35
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.840
[George] A ton of gold.

36
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.920
[George] The United States was in the grip of the brutal economic depression following the Panic of 1893.

37
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:27.120
[George] To millions of unemployed workers, bankrupt farmers and clerks, the news was an irresistible summons to fortune.

38
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:40.360
[George] George Carmack, along with his native Tajish companions Cache and Ka Gooks, discovered coarse gold nuggets lying like cheese pebbles in the gravel of Bonanza Creek, igniting the wildest gold rush in modern history.

39
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:47.720
[George] The announcement unleashed the Klondike Gold Rush, the most frantic, desperate mass stampede in human history.

40
00:05:47.720 --> 00:06:03.240
[George] Over 100,000 people from all walks of life, doctors, lawyers, mayors, waiters, and shopkeepers, abandoned their careers and rushed to Pacific ports to buy tickets on any vessel that could float, from luxury steamers to rotting wooden barges.

41
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:11.480
[George] Arriving at the muddy Alaskan boomtones of Skagway and Daya, the stampeders faced the terrifying physical barrier of the coast mountains.

42
00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:18.520
[George] To reach the gold fields in the Yukon interior, travelers had to cross the Chilcoot Pass or the White Pass on foot.

43
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:27.400
[George] The Northwest Mounted Police in Canada enforced a strict survival regulation to prevent mass starvation in the subarctic wilderness.

44
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:36.440
[George] Every single stampeder was required to bring one ton of food and equipment, a full year's supply, before being allowed to cross the Canadian border.

45
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.080
[George] This meant that each gold seeker had to haul 50 pound packs up the sheer.

46
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.560
[George] Icy stairs of the Chilcoot pass forty times in freezing blizzards.

47
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:53.240
[George] Climbing through knee deep snow alongside thousands of desperate men in a single file human chain known as the Golden Stairs.

48
00:06:53.240 --> 00:07:07.400
[George] The steamship Portland docked in Seattle carrying 68 miners and more than two tons of pure placer gold, sending an electrifying wave of financial hysteria across an America still reeling from the panic of 1893.

49
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:20.880
[George] Those who survived the grueling mountain passes assembled crude wooden rafts and scows from green spruce timber on the shores of Lake Bennett, navigating 500 miles of treacherous, churning rapids down the Yukon River.

50
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:30.560
[George] By 1898, an empty swamp at the confluence of the Klondike and Yukon Rivers was transformed into Dawson City, the Paris of the North.

51
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:39.520
[George] Swelling to 30,000 residents almost overnight, Dawson was a surreal frontier metropolis where miners lit cigars with hundred-dollar bills.

52
00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:46.240
[George] Saloons stayed open 24 hours a day, and dance hall girls danced for bags of gold dust.

53
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:58.960
[George] Prospectors worked through the dark subarctic winter in temperatures of 50 degrees below zero, building wood fires on the ground to thaw the permafrost six inches at a time before shoveling gravel out of 60-foot shafts.

54
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:09.200
[George] When the spring thaw arrived in May 1898, they washed the gravel in wooden sluice boxes, extracting tens of millions of dollars in gleaming gold nuggets.

55
00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.200
[George] Fortunes were made in days.

56
00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] A single shovel of gravel from Bonanza Creek or Eldorado Creek often yielded $800 in gold.

57
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:31.200
[George] Mounties at the summit of Chilcoot Pass required every stampeders to carry a full year's supply of food and equipment, forcing men to climb the golden stairs thirty times in sub-zero blizzards to haul their one-ton outfits.

58
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:40.000
[George] The Klondike frenzy subsided after just a few years as the richest surface gravels were exhausted and consolidated by industrial mining syndicates.

59
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.400
[George] But the prospectors did not go home.

60
00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.920
[George] Instead, they surged west across Alaska to new, even larger gold discoveries.

61
00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] In 1899, gold was discovered along the sandy ocean beaches of Nome, triggering an astonishing spectacle where thousands of miners rocked gold pans directly in the surf of the Bering Sea.

62
00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:09.720
[George] In 1902, Italian immigrant Felix Pedro struck rich gold near Fairbanks, establishing the permanent interior capital of the territory.

63
00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:21.240
[George] Steamships, river sternwheelers, telegraph lines, and railroads laced the wilderness, transforming Alaska from an isolated frozen territory into a vibrant resource giant.

64
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:31.960
[George] Dawson City transformed in months from a swampy moose pasture into the Paris of the North, where dance hall singers were weighed against pure gold dust on brass saloon scales.

65
00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:46.120
[George] Over the next century, Alaska yielded not only billions of dollars in gold, copper and platinum, but an unimaginable treasure in timber, commercial fisheries, and the colossal oil reserves of the North Slope at Prudhoe Bay.

66
00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:56.040
[George] William Seward's $7 million purchase, once the butt of national jokes, was celebrated as the greatest diplomatic and economic coup in American history.

67
00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:06.360
[George] When Seward was asked late in life what he considered his greatest achievement in public service, the old statesman smiled and replied quietly, the purchase of Alaska.

68
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.560
[George] But it will take the people a generation to find it out.

69
00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:23.640
[George] Today, Alaska stands as America's majestic last frontier, a colossal land of glaciers, mountains and boundless riches, forever vindicating the vision of the Secretary of State who looked at a map of snow and saw an empire.

70
00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:38.360
[George] Seward vision proved extraordinarily prophetic, as Alaska yielded billions of dollars in gold, copper, salmon, timber, and oil, establishing the Great Northern Territory as America's greatest strategic acquisition.

71
00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:45.000
[Rebecca] Seward's folly and the Klondike Gold Rush remind us that true visionaries are often misunderstood by their contemporaries.

72
00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:53.040
[Rebecca] It proves that what appears barren and frozen to the short sighted may hold unimaginable riches for those with the patience to explore.

73
00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.000
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

74
00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

75
00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:03.440
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we witness the heroic dogs and mushers of the 1925 Serum Run to Nome.

76
00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.560
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

